def-cesaro-mean :: The Encyclopedia of Mathematics defines the arithmetic averages (s_0+...+s_n)/(n+1) and summability by their limit. This matches the local zero-based definition and direction.
thm-cesaro-mean-theorem :: The source states Cauchy’s theorem that s_n tending to s implies its arithmetic averages tend to s, with the same quantifiers and conclusion. The numbered head-tail proof is local.
ex-rational-function-field-order :: The source gives exactly the eventual-sign/leading-coefficient order on R(t), states that t exceeds every constant, and identifies the field as non-Archimedean. The local proof expands representative independence and the infinitesimal 1/t.
ex-stolz-cesaro-applied :: Faulhaber’s formula has leading term n^(p+1)/(p+1), which gives exactly the displayed limit, and Stolz-Cesaro supplies the stated difference-quotient route. The local proof expands the power factorization.
def-function-limit :: Lebl defines a function limit at a cluster point by the same punctured epsilon-delta quantifiers and explicitly excludes the value at c. The domain, hypotheses, direction, and conclusion match.
lem-function-limit-unique :: Lebl states uniqueness of the limit at a cluster point with the same hypotheses and conclusion. The local proof is independently decomposed into numbered steps.
thm-sequential-criterion-for-function-limits :: Lebl states the same Heine criterion for every sequence in the punctured domain converging to c. The local proof adapts the standard contrapositive construction and exposes its countable-choice step.
lem-limit-implies-local-boundedness :: Lebl states that a function with a finite limit is bounded on a punctured neighborhood, with the standard |L|+1 bound. The local proof writes the estimate directly.
thm-algebra-of-function-limits :: Lebl states the sum, scalar, product, reciprocal, and quotient laws for function limits with the same nonzero denominator-limit hypothesis. The local quotient is carefully restricted to its actual domain.
lem-function-limit-preserves-order :: Lebl states that an eventual non-strict inequality passes to finite function limits, with a non-strict conclusion. The local theorem has the same hypotheses, direction, and conclusion.
thm-squeeze-for-function-limits :: The sources state the squeeze theorem with two equal outer limits and pointwise inequalities near the point, producing the middle limit. The local statement adds an explicit punctured-neighborhood radius.
thm-two-sided-limit-iff-both-one-sided :: The sources state exactly that the two-sided limit exists iff the left and right limits both exist and agree, with the common value. The local item states the side-limit-point well-posedness explicitly.
lem-integer-part :: The source states that for every real x there is a unique integer m with m<=x<m+1, denoted floor(x). The local proof supplies the Archimedean and well-ordering construction.
ex-limit-of-a-polynomial :: The sources state that every real polynomial is continuous, hence its limit at c is its value P(c), with the same finite-degree setting. The local proof derives the claim from its algebra-of-limits dependency.
ex-one-sided-limits-of-the-sign-function :: The sources give the sign function and its left and right limits -1 and 1 at zero, hence failure of the two-sided limit. The local domain and zero-value convention do not affect the punctured calculation.
cex-dirichlet-has-no-limit-anywhere :: The source defines the rational indicator and states that it is discontinuous everywhere because rationals and irrationals are dense, equivalently its punctured limit exists nowhere. The local proof uses the exact two-sequence formulation.

