 {"id":"ex-field-ordered-in-two-ways","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"established-knowledge","urls":[],"rationale":"ESCALATED TO ALPHA. The two orders on Q(sqrt(2)) obtained from the two real embeddings are a standard algebraic example, and the local proof also shows these are the only two. Searches surfaced informal exact discussions but no reputable source among the allowed classes for the full exact package; this is established mathematics, not an invented construction.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
 {"id":"ex-cauchy-complete-not-complete-field","statement":"ai-generated","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"trivial","urls":[],"rationale":"Positive novelty determination: the exact six-row inventory for this library’s K=R((t^{-1})) is a bespoke composition of named local Laurent and completeness results. Each row follows directly from those dependencies; the separate use of an ai-generated dependency is reported as a citation-eligibility defect, not treated as support.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
 {"id":"ex-cesaro-means-of-alternating","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ces%C3%A0ro_summation","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Grandi%27s_series"],"rationale":"The sources give Cesaro averaging and the classical alternating pattern. The local item uses the term sequence 1,-1,... rather than Grandi partial sums, changes the Cesaro value accordingly to 0, and writes out the parity computation.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
-{"id":"ex-stolz-cesaro-applied","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Faulhaber%27s_formula","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stolz%E2%80%93Ces%C3%A0ro_theorem"],"rationale":"Faulhaber’s formula has leading term n^(p+1)/(p+1), which gives exactly the displayed limit, and Stolz-Cesaro supplies the stated difference-quotient route. The local proof expands the power factorization.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
+{"id":"ex-stolz-cesaro-applied","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Faulhaber%27s_formula","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stolz%E2%80%93Ces%C3%A0ro_theorem"],"rationale":"The local example composes two sourced results rather than reproducing one source statement: Faulhaber’s formula gives leading term n^(p+1)/(p+1), hence the displayed limit, and Stolz-Cesaro supplies the stated difference-quotient route. The local proof expands the power factorization.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
 {"id":"cex-q-not-dense-in-an-ordered-field","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Archimedean_property","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ordered_field"],"rationale":"The sources state that a non-Archimedean ordered field has infinitesimal intervals containing no rational and give R(t) as the standard example. The local witness fixes the interval (0,1/t) and verifies it from the canonical embedding.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
 {"id":"cex-evt-and-ivt-fail-over-a-non-complete-field","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"established-knowledge","urls":[],"rationale":"ESCALATED TO ALPHA. Failure of IVT and EVT over Q through the missing root sqrt(2), including unbounded reciprocal and unattained extremum witnesses, is standard analysis over an incomplete ordered field. Searches found the component theorems but no allowed reputable source for this exact three-function package; it is established mathematics, not positively novel.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
 {"id":"cex-rolle-fails-over-a-non-complete-field","statement":"ai-generated","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"trivial","urls":[],"rationale":"Positive novelty determination: the exact locally constant two-valued witness assembled from the rational cut at sqrt(2) is a bespoke companion to the preceding item. Its endpoint equality, continuity, and absence of a zero derivative are checked directly in the numbered proof.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
@@ -54,11 +54,11 @@
 {"id":"ex-limit-of-a-polynomial","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Polynomial","https://www.jirka.org/ra/html/sec_limoffunc.html"],"rationale":"The sources state that every real polynomial is continuous, hence its limit at c is its value P(c), with the same finite-degree setting. The local proof derives the claim from its algebra-of-limits dependency.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
 {"id":"ex-distance-to-the-integers","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Triangle_wave","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Floor_and_ceiling_functions"],"rationale":"The sources identify the distance-to-nearest-integer triangle wave and the floor convention. The local item packages attainment, range, zero set, half-integer values, and period one in one worked example.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
 {"id":"ex-x-times-psi-tends-to-zero","statement":"ai-generated","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"trivial","urls":[],"rationale":"Positive novelty determination: replacing sin(1/x) by this library’s bespoke distance-to-integers psi and damping it by x is a repository-specific witness. The bound 0<=psi<=1/2 immediately gives |x psi(1/x)|<=|x|/2 and the squeeze proof verifies the limit.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
-{"id":"ex-one-sided-limits-of-the-sign-function","statement":"literature-derived","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"exact-source","urls":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sign_function","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/One-sided_limit"],"rationale":"The sources give the sign function and its left and right limits -1 and 1 at zero, hence failure of the two-sided limit. The local domain and zero-value convention do not affect the punctured calculation.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
+{"id":"ex-one-sided-limits-of-the-sign-function","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sign_function","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/One-sided_limit"],"rationale":"The local example combines the sourced sign-function convention with the sourced one-sided-limit criterion; these imply the left and right limits -1 and 1 at zero and hence failure of the two-sided limit. The value assigned at zero does not affect the punctured calculation.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
 {"id":"ex-limit-at-infinity-of-a-rational-function","statement":"ai-generated","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"trivial","urls":[],"rationale":"Positive novelty determination: the exact rational function (3x^2-1)/(x^2+x) and the bound |f(x)-3|<=4/x were selected as a bespoke direct-estimate worksheet for the local infinity definition. The displayed algebra and Archimedean estimate verify it.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-08"}
