items/lem-limit-implies-local-boundedness.md-50-
items/lem-limit-implies-local-boundedness.md:51:**Given:** A set $A \subseteq \mathbb{R}$, a limit point $c$ of $A$, a function $f : A \to \mathbb{R}$ and a real $L$ with $\lim_{x \to c} f(x) = L$ ([[def-function-limit]], [[def-limit-point-r]]).
items/lem-limit-implies-local-boundedness.md-52-
items/lem-limit-implies-local-boundedness.md-53-[L1] The limit condition: for every real $\varepsilon > 0$ there is a real $\delta > 0$ such that every $x \in A$ with $0 < |x - c| < \delta$ satisfies $|f(x) - L| < \varepsilon$ ([[def-function-limit]]).
items/lem-limit-implies-local-boundedness.md-54-
items/lem-limit-implies-local-boundedness.md-55-[L2] Absolute value: $|u| \ge 0$; $-|u| \le u \le |u|$; and for $t > 0$, $|u| \le t$ is equivalent to $-t \le u \le t$ ([[lem-of-abs-value]]).
items/lem-limit-implies-local-boundedness.md-56-
items/lem-limit-implies-local-boundedness.md-57-[L3] Triangle inequality: $|u + v| \le |u| + |v|$ ([[lem-of-triangle-inequality]]).
items/lem-limit-implies-local-boundedness.md-58-
items/lem-limit-implies-local-boundedness.md:59:[L4] Order arithmetic: $0 < 1$ ([[cor-of-one-positive]]); adding a constant preserves the order and adding inequalities is legitimate ([[lem-of-add-order]]); and $u < v$ implies $u \le v$. [[lem-of-add-order]] states these moves in their STRICT forms only; the non-strict forms used below follow by adjoining the equality case, in which the two sides coincide, the order being total ([[def-ordered-field]]).
items/lem-limit-implies-local-boundedness.md-60-
items/lem-limit-implies-local-boundedness.md:61:[L5] Bounded set: $S \subseteq \mathbb{R}$ is bounded when it has both an upper and a lower bound ([[def-bounded-set]]); and $N^{*}_{\delta}(c) = \{\, y : 0 < |y - c| < \delta \,\}$ ([[def-neighbourhood-r]]).
