items/ex-cauchy-complete-not-complete-field.md-62-[L3] Every Cauchy sequence in $K$ converges in $K$ ([[thm-laurent-cauchy-complete]]), which is (CC) ([[def-completeness-properties]], [[def-sequences-in-an-ordered-field]]).
items/ex-cauchy-complete-not-complete-field.md-63-
items/ex-cauchy-complete-not-complete-field.md-64-[L4] The set $A = \{\, n \cdot 1_K : n \in \mathbb{N}\,\}$ is nonempty, bounded above by $t$, and has no least upper bound in $K$, so (LUB) fails ([[cor-laurent-not-lub-complete]]).
items/ex-cauchy-complete-not-complete-field.md-65-
items/ex-cauchy-complete-not-complete-field.md:66:[L5] Every nested sequence of closed intervals of $K$ whose lengths tend to $0$ in $K$ has exactly one common point, so (NIP) holds; the unrestricted form fails ([[cor-laurent-nested-intervals]], [[cex-laurent-nested-intervals-empty]]).
items/ex-cauchy-complete-not-complete-field.md-67-
items/ex-cauchy-complete-not-complete-field.md-68-[L6] (BW) implies the Archimedean property ([[lem-bw-implies-archimedean]]) and so does (MCT) ([[lem-mct-implies-archimedean]]); and the five properties are equivalent once the Archimedean property is supplied where needed ([[thm-completeness-equivalences]]).
